 ## Subtopics
 
-### Fourier PlenOctree
-A method designed for human silhouette rendering using Shape-From-Silhouette, storing colors and densities on a coarse PlenOctree and using a Fourier Transform MLP [S3]. It constructs a coarse visual hull using sparse views predicted from a generalized NeRF and Shape-From-Silhouette, then densely samples colors and densities inside this hull to store on a coarse PlenOctree [S3]. After sampling dense views from the PlenOctree and applying transmissivity thresholding to eliminate most empty points, it generates new leaf densities and spherical harmonic (SH) color coefficients for remaining points and updates the PlenOctree [S3]. Finally, it uses a Fourier Transform MLP to extract Fourier coefficients of the density and SH color coefficients, which are fed into an inverse discrete Fourier transform to restore the SH coefficients and density [S3].
+### [PlenOctree](1366-plenoctree.md)
+A sparse octree-based 3D representation that enables real-time rendering of NeRFs by pre-tabulating view-dependent radiance into a voxel structure. Unlike naive NeRF rendering which requires dense sampling and neural network inference for every sample dependent on viewing direction, PlenOctrees remove the need for neural network queries at test time by storing precomputed spherical harmonic coefficients. It achieves inference speeds approximately 3000 times faster than the original NeRF implementation.
+
+### [Neural Codebook](11912-neural-codebook.md)
+A shallow neural network module designed to recover high-frequency spatial details lost during the non-uniform compression and downsampling of voxel grid models (specifically density and color features) in radiance field optimization. It acts as a refinement step that maps a compressed, upsampled grid back to the original resolution, restoring fidelity without the storage overhead of dense grids. Unlike sibling methods like [PlenOctree](1366-plenoctree.md) which rely on sparse octree structures for storage, NCB operates on dense grids to provide a compact, high-quality representation suitable for real-time rendering.
+
+### [Fourier PlenOctree](11976-fourier-plenoctree.md)
+A 4D scene representation designed for real-time rendering of free-viewpoint videos by compressing time-variant information in the frequency domain. It extends the static PlenOctree [1366-plenoctree.md] by storing Fourier coefficients for density and spherical harmonic (SH) color coefficients within an octree structure, allowing the reconstruction of dynamic scenes from a unified spatial grid. Unlike standard PlenOctree where structures vary per frame, Fourier PlenOctree requires a unified octree structure across all timestamps to analyze plenoptic functions at consistent positions.
+
+### [PlenOctree Fusion](16748-plenoctree-fusion.md)
+A coarse-to-fine strategy to obtain a completed PlenOctree by fusing local PlenOctrees from multiple target views, filtering redundant leaves, and refining leaf values using transmittance-based weighting instead of gradient descent.
+
+### PlenOctrees Visibility Pruning
+Application of visibility-based pruning to PlenOctrees, replacing density-threshold pruning with visibility masks, followed by fine-tuning and compression [S3]. Convert PlenOctree child nodes $p_{oct}$ to voxelized point cloud positions $p_{pc}$ in a voxel grid of depth $R$: $p_{pc} = p_{oct} \times 2^R - 0.5$. Convert local voxel coordinates to world positions and compute the visibility mask $O$ using the general visibility algorithm. Prune voxels based on the visibility mask $O$ and MVE parameter $\nu$. Convert the pruned point cloud $M$ back to a PlenOctree $\hat{G}$. Fine-tune the octree using stochastic gradient descent on training images with NeRF rendering loss: $L_{RGB} = \sum_{r \in R} ||C(r) - \hat{C}(r)||^2$ [S3]. Grid resolution is a parameter: lower resolution increases voxel size to fill holes caused by pruning but reduces detail [S3]. Fixed voxel splats can cause holes if voxel size is too small relative to pruning aggressiveness, or appear blocky if voxel size is too large [S3].
 
